WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.164
llama.cpp can be used as a local inference runtime and, at a high level, as a server that exposes model inference through API endpoints.

00:00:09.164 --> 00:00:18.059
API serving lets web UIs, scripts, applications, and automation call the model without manually running each prompt from a terminal.

00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:23.045
This turns a local model into a service boundary that other tools can use.

00:00:23.045 --> 00:00:28.031
Many local AI tools expect OpenAI-compatible API patterns at a high level.

00:00:28.031 --> 00:00:35.241
Compatibility can make integration easier, but it does not remove the need to understand the local service.

00:00:35.241 --> 00:00:48.448
Operators still need to know which model is loaded, what context length is configured, what endpoint is reachable, how requests are logged, and whether streaming or structured output is supported.

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:51.143
Network exposure should be conservative.

00:00:51.143 --> 00:01:00.846
Local-only binding is a safer default for a private inference server because it reduces the chance that unintended users can reach the endpoint.

00:01:00.846 --> 00:01:09.000
LAN access, reverse proxies, authentication, and TLS may be useful in shared environments, but they should be deliberate.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.245
Avoid accidental public exposure of private inference services.

00:01:13.245 --> 00:01:17.827
Prompts and outputs should be treated as potentially sensitive data.

00:01:17.827 --> 00:01:25.306
They may include personal notes, source code, documents, secrets, business information, or operational details.

00:01:25.306 --> 00:01:34.201
If an API server is connected to a web UI or application, think about logging, retention, access control, and who can send requests.

00:01:34.201 --> 00:01:37.570
Serving a model is not just a convenience feature.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.591
It is an operational decision.
